<<<
Hello may perhaps be put aside as American, hillo & hilloa as archaic, & hollow as confusable with another word.
===
*Hello* may perhaps be put aside as American, *hillo* & *hilloa* as archaic, & *hollow* as confusable with another word.
>>>

<<<
the best sclection & arrangement
===
the best selection & arrangement
>>>

<<<
perhaps: Hullo for the interjection & for the noun as the name of the interjection; halloo for the noun as the name of a shout, & for the verb in dignified contexts; holla (with past holla’d) for the verb in colloquial contexts.
===
perhaps: *Hullo* for the interjection & for the noun as the name of the interjection; *halloo* for the noun as the name of a shout, & for the verb in dignified contexts; *holla* (with past *holla’d*) for the verb in colloquial contexts.
>>>

<<<
We thus get:—Hullo! is that you? ; He stopped short with a Hullo; The minstrel heard the far halloo; Do not kalloo until you are out of the wood; He hollad out something that I could not catch,
===
We thus get:—*Hullo! is that you?; He stopped short with a Hullo; The minstrel heard the far halloo; Do not halloo until you are out of the wood; He holla’d out something that I could not catch.*
>>>

<<<
The forms hallo(a), holler, & hollo(a), would thus be got rid of as well as hello, hillo(a), & hollow.
===
The forms *hallo(a), holler,* & *hollo(a),* would thus be got rid of as well as *hello, hillo(a),* & *hollow.*
>>>

